<div class="guide-styles" data-zaf-dynamic="article" id="guide-body" lang="en">Welcome to Wowhead's Phase 3 Best in Slot Gear list for Discipline Priest Healer in Wrath of the Lich King Classic. Gear in this guide is primarily obtained from Trial of the Grand Crusader. This guide will list the recommended gear for your class and role, containing gear sourced from raids, dungeons, PvP, professions, Bind on Equip drops, and reputations.<h2 class="heading-size-2" id="best-in-slot">Best in Slot Gear for Discipline Priest Healer Phase 3</h2>
<i>Healer gearing always heavily depends on playstyle, situation and raid environment so treat the BiS list below as a guideline rather than a gospel.</i>
<br/>
<br/>While there is a variety of strong items that can be considered Phase 3 BiS, naturally some of them are rare hardmode drops or highly sought after pieces that will generally go to caster dps first. Due to that, the gear embed below has both a contested and a more realistic setup to choose from, though most players will likely end up with something inbetween.<br/>
<br/>Another thing worth mentioning is that you effectively cannot avoid going above the hastecap in phase bis setups anymore, since many of our favoured options simply provide haste rating alongside the other stats that already make them the best choice. Nonetheless, there is still some value in the extra haste, which comes from both quicker <a class="icontiny" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/spell=53007/penance">
<img loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/spell_holy_penance.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Penance</span></a>, <a class="icontiny" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/spell=48123/smite">
<img loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/spell_holy_holysmite.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Smite</span></a> and <a class="icontiny" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/spell=48072/prayer-of-healing">
<img loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/spell_holy_prayerofhealing02.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Prayer of Healing</span></a> hardcasts as well as the benefit on any spells cast without <a class="icontiny" data-entity="spell" data-entity-has-icon="true" href="https://www.wowhead.com/wotlk/spell=52800/borrowed-time">
<img loading="lazy" src="https://wow.zamimg.com/images/wow/icons/tiny/spell_holy_borrowedtime.gif" style="vertical-align: middle;"/> <span class="tinyicontxt">Borrowed Time</span></a>.<h3 class="heading-size-3" id="tier-8-versus-tier-9">Tier 8 versus Tier 9</h3>Due to our incredibly strong set bonus from the last phase, there is no reason to go for any setup that does not include T8 4p as a Discipline Priest, which allows us to pass on the contested T9 tokens for the most part. <br/>
<br/>Even using the strongest version of the new tier set and taking both set bonuses into account, opting for T9 4p only provides a moderate amount of stamina and sustain stats in exchange for losing a large chunk of spellpower, which is objectively a net loss. <br/>
<br/>Though some people might consider sacrificing some throughput for additional mana regeneration or survivability, there are already better and more easily available trade-offs for both of these things in the form of alternate trinket or enchant options, so that is not a valid argument for dropping T8 either.<br/>
